      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a bit better, but I want to push the realism even further. To make it truly \"noir,\" the danger needs to feel visceral. When these characters show up, the audience shouldn't just see a person; they should feel a sense of the specific urban decay or \"street\" intensity they represent.\n\nFor the hitman and the fixer, can you lean into how their specific backgrounds make them more intimidating or dangerous to the protagonist? Like, instead of just saying they're charismatic or disciplined, describe how their cultural traits or the environments they come from contribute to that \"threat\" factor. I want to understand how their specific ethnic or street identities make them such a stark, gritty contrast to my clean-cut, academic lead. Give me a bit more on how their \"world\" makes them feel more animalistic or unpredictable compared to the detective's orderly life.",
